## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses **shallow equality** (`===`) to decide if props changed. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference.

```jsx
// This creates a NEW object on every render — child always re-renders
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// This creates a NEW function on every render — same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of the parent produces a new reference, so the child sees "new" props even if the data is identical.

---

### 2. The Three Tools

**`React.memo`** — skips re-rendering a component if its props are shallowly equal.
- Use it on **pure components** that render often with the same props.
- Useless without also stabilizing the props you pass (see below).

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a **function** across renders.
- Use when passing callbacks as props to memoized children.
- Dependency array works identically to `useEffect`.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a **computed value** across renders.
- Use for **expensive calculations** or to stabilize an object/array reference passed as a prop.
- Don't use it for cheap computations — the memoization itself has cost.

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | component render | child is pure, props are stable |
| `useCallback` | function reference | passing callbacks to memo'd children |
| `useMemo` | computed value | expensive calc or stable object/array ref |

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders whenever the context **value reference** changes — even if the data inside is the same.

```jsx
// BAD — new object on every render, all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

```jsx
// GOOD — stable reference
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Another pattern: **split context** — separate a `UserContext` (changes often) from a `UserDispatchContext` (stable `setUser`). Components that only dispatch never re-render on user changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ active: true });

  // New function reference every render
  const handleSelect = (item) => console.log(item);

  // New object reference every render
  const options = { filters, limit: 50 };

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList options={options} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ options, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('rendered'); // fires on every keystroke
  return <ul>...</ul>;
};
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filters` actually changes:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ active: true });

  // Stable reference — only changes if filters changes
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => console.log(item), []);

  // Stable object reference — only changes if filters changes
  const options = useMemo(() => ({ filters, limit: 50 }), [filters]);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList options={options} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Now React.memo can do its job
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ options, onSelect }) => {
  console.log('rendered'); // only fires when filters changes
  return <ul>...</ul>;
});
```

---

### Key Heuristic

Apply these optimizations **after** you measure with React DevTools Profiler — premature memoization adds complexity and can itself hurt performance if the dependency arrays are heavy. The pattern `React.memo` + `useCallback`/`useMemo` only pays off when the memoized child is genuinely expensive to render.
